{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Load the supplied HR LDIF into LDAP on node1 and preserve its employee data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node1 administrator search directly returns the supplied Morgan Lake record, mail address, and employee number before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f542acb4eff949a8","cmd-2d1fd0402817492b","cmd-bcb4d82fd0814bdc"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow the directory-reader identity to search employee names and mail from node2 without reading employeeNumber.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Authenticated searches from node2 return cn, sn, and mail while omitting employeeNumber, both before and after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0732fafd90644aab","cmd-ac0d2d6c790d40b8","cmd-4398e1269e9144fa","cmd-a17fb5079bd240ee"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Permit only the HR administrator to read or change employeeNumber.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The ACL grants the HR administrator write access and denies all others; the reader receives LDAP error 50 on modification, while the administrator reads, changes, and restores the value successfully. The restriction persists after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b8dbeb0e7c704f53","cmd-68c5a59ad6a349f6","cmd-e72cff88dc084513","cmd-bcb4d82fd0814bdc","cmd-a17fb5079bd240ee"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Prevent anonymous users from seeing employee records.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Anonymous subtree searches from node2 succeed but return zero employee entries before and after the restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0732fafd90644aab","cmd-ac0d2d6c790d40b8","cmd-4398e1269e9144fa","cmd-a17fb5079bd240ee"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the supplied records under /srv/legacy2/ldap-attribute-privacy.","status":"satisfied","summary":"hr.ldif remains at the required path with the original 201-byte content represented in LDAP and remains present with a stable executor-observed SHA-1 checksum after node1 reboot.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-f542acb4eff949a8","cmd-0a84291badbf41d4","cmd-2d1fd0402817492b","cmd-bcb4d82fd0814bdc"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Provision dedicated demonstration credentials for the reader and HR administrator.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Separate generated password files and bind-identity documentation were created. Password files are root-owned and mode 600, and the node2 demonstration copies persist after reboot with protected permissions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0a84291badbf41d4","cmd-7f9e904199384cfc","cmd-4398e1269e9144fa"]},{"id":"R7","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remain on CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 after execution and reboot. Signed CentOS 5 OpenLDAP 2.3.43 packages were installed and shown operating successfully.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f007e388e6114eda","cmd-967ff922b70b42e5","cmd-b5bc073d5b2e4b5c","cmd-bcb4d82fd0814bdc","cmd-4398e1269e9144fa","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.75,"summary":"The final task state is functional and restricted by LDAP ACLs and a node2-specific firewall rule. Two unnecessary troubleshooting mutations occurred: cmd-92e170787b2f4810 installed a temporary SSH private key on node1, and cmd-a6f9b5de685947d2 appended temporary authorization on node2; the attempted SSH transfer did not contribute to the final transfer method. These were reverted by cmd-55ad01a52b1244a3 and cmd-1b578ff11fa3400f. Credential distribution then used a temporary cleartext HTTP server and firewall opening (cmd-43853c4174664aa0); it contributed to provisioning and was source-restricted and fully removed by cmd-73fa5176ebb44c78, but created a bounded security risk. Necessary RPM downloads and reboot logs were not cleaned: lifecycle snapshots show three RPMs plus managed-reboot.log remaining on node1 and one RPM plus managed-reboot.log on node2. No executor-caused failed-service or package-health regression is established.","evidence_ids":["cmd-92e170787b2f4810","cmd-a6f9b5de685947d2","cmd-55ad01a52b1244a3","cmd-1b578ff11fa3400f","cmd-43853c4174664aa0","cmd-73fa5176ebb44c78","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct access-control and persistence tests. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered service or client functionality, credentials, data, ACL behavior, and platform state. Hygiene is reduced by a reverted SSH-transfer experiment, the temporary cleartext credential-transfer endpoint, and abandoned files in /tmp.","limitations":["Baseline preservation used SHA-256 and POSIX cksum while final checks used SHA-1, so unchanged source-file identity is supported by stable path, size, displayed content, and LDAP data rather than a directly comparable checksum.","The legacy package-health lifecycle probe invokes an unsupported yum operation and cannot establish final dependency consistency; successful RPM queries and running services provide narrower evidence instead.","The generic after-executor listening-socket snapshots are empty even though direct privileged netstat and service checks show slapd listening after reboot; the reason for this collector discrepancy is not captured.","Systemd failed-unit collection is unsupported on this legacy platform, so service health rests on direct SysV service, process, listener, and functional LDAP checks."]}
